.TH KILLBILL.CONF 5 "2026-09-09" "killbill-rs 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
killbill.conf, config.toml \- configuration for killbilld
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/killbill/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR killbilld (8)
daemon reads a single TOML file, by default
.IR /etc/killbill/config.toml .
The daemon owns writes to it:
.BR killbillctl (1)
and
.BR killbill-tui (1)
request changes and the daemon validates and persists them, so the running
state and the file can never drift. A daemon-side write re-serializes the file
and
.B comments and formatting are lost;
run
.B killbillctl reload
after editing by hand.
.PP
Validation is
.BR "fail-closed" .
The daemon collects
.I every
problem, reports them all, and
.B refuses to arm
until the file is valid.
.B Any unrecognized key is a hard error
\(en a typo disables protection loudly rather than being silently ignored.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [general]
.TP
.B armed_at_boot
Boolean, default
.BR false .
Arm automatically when the daemon starts. A fresh install ships this
.B false
so installation can never lock you out. Armed state is otherwise never
persisted: after a crash or restart the daemon comes back disarmed unless this
is set.
.SS [detection]
.TP
.B sensors
Array of strings, default
.BR [\(dqusb\(dq] .
Which sensors to run. v1 implements only
.BR \(dqusb\(dq
(kernel netlink uevents). The list is what makes the sensor layer pluggable; an
unknown name is a validation error.
.PP
While armed, a root-issued synthetic removal uevent
.RB ( "udevadm trigger --action=remove" )
fires the power action for the affected device, exactly like a physical unplug.
A synthetic
.I add
re-announcing an already-connected device is recorded but never acted on.
.SS [[whitelist]]
Repeat this block once per device allowed to be connected while armed.
.TP
.B id
String, required. The USB
.I vendor:product
pair in lowercase hex, as
.BR lsusb (8)
prints \(en for example
.BR \(dq1050:0407\(dq .
Malformed ids are a validation error.
.TP
.B label
String, optional. A human-readable name, shown in
.B killbillctl
output. Cosmetic only.
.TP
.B max_count
Integer, default
.BR 1 .
How many copies of this
.B id
may be connected at once. A whitelisted device added beyond this count fires.
.IP
.B killbilld
counts only devices whose
.I add
event it has seen since it started \(en it does
.B not
enumerate already-connected USB devices at startup (a documented v1
limitation). A device present before
.B killbilld
started therefore counts as zero. To seed the table before arming, run
.RS
.EX
udevadm trigger --action=add --subsystem-match=usb
.EE
.RE
while disarmed. The resulting synthetic
.I add
events are recorded and never acted on. Do not run it while armed with
.B on_sensor_gap
set to
.BR kill ,
where the event burst can overflow the netlink receive buffer and fire a real
.B SensorGap
kill.
.PP
The whitelist suppresses
.I additions
you expect. It never makes a device safe to
.IR remove :
unplugging
.B any
device while armed fires, whitelisted or not \(en an unplug is the classic
seizure signal.
.SS [response]
.TP
.B dry_run
Boolean, default
.BR false .
When true, a kill decision is logged and broadcast but no power action or
destruction is performed. Use this first when setting up.
.TP
.B power_action
String, default
.BR \(dqpoweroff\(dq .
One of:
.RS
.TP
.B poweroff
Cut power. The LUKS disk re-locks. This is the point of the tool.
.TP
.B halt
Stop the CPU but leave power on. RAM keeps the LUKS master key and the disk
never re-locks \(en weak against the seizure threat model. Use only if you know
why.
.TP
.B none
Detect and log only; take no power action.
.RE
.TP
.B lock_screen_first
Boolean, default
.BR false .
Must be
.BR false .
Setting it
.B true
makes the daemon
.B refuse to arm:
a pre-poweroff wait would delay the kill path, and a fire-and-forget lock never
completes before power is cut. The LUKS re-lock already covers a seized machine.
.TP
.B on_sensor_gap
String, default
.BR \(dqwarn\(dq .
What to do if the USB sensor loses events (kernel receive-buffer overflow, an
unparseable datagram, or the sensor thread crashing) \(en for example a device
storm meant to bury a real add or remove:
.RS
.TP
.B warn
Surface it in
.B killbillctl status
.RB ( events_lost )
and refuse to (re)arm until the daemon is restarted.
.TP
.B kill
Treat the gap as an unauthorized change and fire
.BR power_action .
A gap fires the power action
.BR only ;
it never triggers
.B luks_destroy
even when that is configured.
.RE
An unrecognized value is a hard validation error.
.SS [response.luks_destroy]
.B Absent by default and deliberately commented out in the shipped example.
LUKS header destruction. In v1 the responder is a
.B stub:
it logs what it would do and
.B wipes nothing.
Enabling it still requires the spelled-out acknowledgment key below (there is no
generic
.BR "enabled = true" ), and the daemon refuses to arm unless the target resolves to a
block device.
.TP
.B i_understand_this_is_irreversible
Boolean. Must be
.B true
for the block to be accepted.
.TP
.B target_header
String. An absolute path to a block device under
.IR /dev .
The daemon checks at arm time that it exists and is a block device, and refuses
to arm otherwise. Under the shipped systemd unit,
.B PrivateDevices=yes
hides the real
.IR /dev ,
so a valid target reports as missing until the
.I killbilld.service.d/luks-destroy.conf
drop-in is installed \(en see
.BR killbilld (8).
.PP
There is a
.I second,
runtime opt-in on top of this:
.B killbillctl luks engage on
(or the TUI's fenced
.B DESTROY
screen). Both the config acknowledgment and the runtime engage must be set
before a kill would invoke the (stubbed) destruction.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
[general]
armed_at_boot = false

[detection]
sensors = ["usb"]

[[whitelist]]
id = "1050:0407"
label = "YubiKey 5C"
max_count = 1

[response]
dry_run = true
power_action = "poweroff"
lock_screen_first = false
on_sensor_gap = "warn"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR killbilld (8),
.BR killbillctl (1),
.BR killbill-tui (1),
.BR lsusb (8)
.PP
A fully-commented reference config is installed at
.IR /usr/share/killbill-rs/config.example.toml .
